{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"nvidiaTeslaT4","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":16365661}],"dockerImageVersionId":31401,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":true}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# DINOv2 + Mask2Former-Swin-S Implementation\n\n## Objective\n\nThe objective of this implementation is to perform landslide segmentation using a transformer-based semantic segmentation architecture. The model combines the feature representation capability of DINOv2 with the segmentation capability of Mask2Former using a Swin-Small backbone. The goal is to accurately identify landslide regions from InSAR-derived coherence and phase information.\n\n---\n\n# Dataset Description\n\nThe implementation uses the ISSLIDE dataset consisting of:\n\n- Coherence Images\n- Phase Images\n- Ground Truth Segmentation Masks\n\nEach sample contains:\n\n- Coherence Image (.tif)\n- Phase Image (.tif)\n- Segmentation Mask (.tif)\n\nThe dataset is organized region-wise and contains multiple temporal observations.\n\n---\n\n# Input Feature Construction\n\nThree channels are generated for every sample.\n\n## Channel 1: Coherence\n\nThe coherence image represents the similarity between SAR acquisitions.\n\n```text\nChannel 1 = Coherence\n```\n\n## Channel 2: Cosine of Phase\n\nPhase values are transformed into cosine representation.\n\n```text\nChannel 2 = cos(Phase)\n```\n\n## Channel 3: Sine of Phase\n\nPhase values are transformed into sine representation.\n\n```text\nChannel 3 = sin(Phase)\n```\n\n## Final Input Tensor\n\n```text\nInput Shape\n\n[3 × H × W]\n\n=\n\n[\n Coherence,\n Cos(Phase),\n Sin(Phase)\n]\n```\n\n---\n\n# Preprocessing\n\n## Z-Score Normalization\n\nEach channel is normalized independently using:\n\n```math\nz = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}\n```\n\n![{482BD1FC-D5D5-4D5A-BFBD-D892935C5367}.png](attachment:b91975e0-7135-4cc4-ac23-2d01e0a2a4ba.png)\n\nWhere:\n\n- μ = Channel Mean\n- σ = Channel Standard Deviation\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Stabilizes training\n- Reduces feature scale variation\n- Improves convergence\n- Helps transformer models learn efficiently\n\n---\n\n# Data Augmentation\n\nTo improve model generalization and reduce overfitting, the following augmentations were applied during training.\n\n## Horizontal Flip\n\n```text\nProbability = 0.5\n```\n\n## Vertical Flip\n\n```text\nProbability = 0.5\n```\n\n## Random Rotation\n\nImplemented using:\n\n```text\nRandomRotate90\n```\n\nwhich automatically generates:\n\n```text\n0°\n90°\n180°\n270°\n```\n\nrotations.\n\n## Transpose\n\n```text\nImage → Imageᵀ\n```\n\nProbability:\n\n```text\n0.5\n```\n\n### Purpose\n\n- Increase training diversity\n- Improve orientation invariance\n- Improve robustness to geometric variations\n\n---\n\n# Dataset Split\n\nThe dataset was divided into:\n\n```text\nTraining Set   = 70%\nValidation Set = 15%\nTesting Set    = 15%\n```\n\nusing:\n\n```python\nrandom_split()\n```\n\n---\n\n# Data Loaders\n\n## Training DataLoader\n\n```text\nBatch Size = 8\nShuffle    = True\n```\n\n## Validation DataLoader\n\n```text\nBatch Size = 8\nShuffle    = False\n```\n\n## Testing DataLoader\n\n```text\nBatch Size = 8\nShuffle    = False\n```\n\n---\n\n# Model Architecture\n\n## Feature Extractor\n\n### DINOv2-Base\n\nModel:\n\n```text\nfacebook/dinov2-base\n```\n\nApproximate Parameters:\n\n```text\n86 Million Parameters\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Self-supervised pretraining\n- Strong feature representation\n- Transformer-based architecture\n- Effective transfer learning capability\n\n---\n\n## Segmentation Network\n\n### Mask2Former-Swin-S\n\nModel:\n\n```text\nfacebook/mask2former-swin-small-ade-semantic\n```\n\nApproximate Parameters:\n\n```text\n69 Million Parameters\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Mask Attention Mechanism\n- Multi-scale Feature Learning\n- Query-based Segmentation\n- Strong Semantic Segmentation Performance\n\n---\n\n# Total Model Size\n\nApproximate model parameters:\n\n```text\nDINOv2 Base        ≈ 86M\nMask2Former Swin-S ≈ 69M\n\nTotal              ≈ 155M Parameters\n```\n\n---\n\n# Loss Functions\n\nA hybrid loss function was used to handle class imbalance and improve segmentation quality.\n\n## Dice Loss\n\nOptimizes segmentation overlap.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Suitable for imbalanced datasets\n- Directly optimizes segmentation quality\n\n---\n\n## Focal Loss\n\nDesigned to focus on difficult samples.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Handles class imbalance\n- Reduces easy negative dominance\n- Improves boundary learning\n\n---\n\n## Tversky Loss\n\nA generalized Dice loss.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Penalizes false negatives\n- Improves landslide detection performance\n- Suitable for rare object segmentation\n\n---\n\n## Final Hybrid Loss\n\n```text\nLoss =\n0.3 × Dice Loss\n+\n0.2 × Focal Loss\n+\n0.5 × Tversky Loss\n```\n\n---\n\n# Optimizer\n\nOptimizer used:\n\n```text\nAdamW\n```\n\nConfiguration:\n\n```text\nLearning Rate = 5e-5\nWeight Decay  = 1e-4\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Stable transformer optimization\n- Better regularization\n- Improved convergence\n\n---\n\n# Learning Rate Scheduler\n\nScheduler:\n\n```text\nReduceLROnPlateau\n```\n\nConfiguration:\n\n```text\nMode      = Min\nFactor    = 0.5\nPatience  = 5\n```\n\n### Purpose\n\nReduce learning rate when validation loss stops improving.\n\n---\n\n# Early Stopping\n\nEarly stopping was implemented to prevent overfitting.\n\n```text\nPatience = 15\n```\n\nTraining stops when:\n\n```text\nValidation Loss\ndoes not improve\nfor 15 consecutive epochs\n```\n\n### Benefits\n\n- Prevents overfitting\n- Saves computational resources\n- Retains best model checkpoint\n\n---\n\n# Evaluation Metrics\n\nThe following metrics were used for performance evaluation.\n\n## Accuracy\n\nMeasures overall pixel-wise classification correctness.\n\n```math\nAccuracy = \\frac{TP + TN}{TP + TN + FP + FN}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Precision\n\nMeasures false positive control.\n\n```math\nPrecision = \\frac{TP}{TP + FP}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Recall\n\nMeasures landslide detection capability.\n\n```math\nRecall = \\frac{TP}{TP + FN}\n```\n\n---\n\n## F1 Score\n\nHarmonic mean of Precision and Recall.\n\n```math\nF1 = \\frac{2TP}{2TP + FP + FN}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Dice Score\n\nMeasures segmentation overlap.\n\n```math\nDice = \\frac{2TP}{2TP + FP + FN}\n```\n\n---\n\n## IoU (Intersection over Union)\n\nMeasures overlap quality between prediction and ground truth.\n\n```math\nIoU = \\frac{TP}{TP + FP + FN}\n```\n\n---\n\n# Visualization\n\nThe following visual outputs were generated.\n\n## Input Image\n\nOriginal grayscale image.\n\n```text\nBlack → Low Intensity\nWhite → High Intensity\n```\n\n---\n\n## Ground Truth Mask\n\nActual landslide labels.\n\n```text\nWhite → Landslide\nBlack → Background\n```\n\n---\n\n## Predicted Mask\n\nModel predictions.\n\n```text\nWhite → Predicted Landslide\nBlack → Background\n```\n\n---\n\n## Probability Heatmap\n\nRaw output before thresholding.\n\n```text\nBlue   → Low Probability\nGreen  → Medium Probability\nYellow → High Probability\nRed    → Very High Probability\n```\n\n---\n\n## Overlay Visualization\n\nCombination of input image and probability heatmap.\n\nPurpose:\n\n- Visual verification\n- Region localization analysis\n- Error inspection\n\n---\n\n# Training Monitoring\n\nThe following graphs were generated:\n\n- Training Loss vs Epoch\n- Validation Loss vs Epoch\n- Training Accuracy vs Epoch\n- Validation Accuracy vs Epoch\n- Training Precision vs Epoch\n- Validation Precision vs Epoch\n- Training Recall vs Epoch\n- Validation Recall vs Epoch\n- Validation F1 Score vs Epoch\n- Validation Dice Score vs Epoch\n- Validation IoU vs Epoch\n\n---\n\n# Final Validation Results\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|----------|----------|\n| Accuracy | 0.9883 |\n| Precision | 0.7659 |\n| Recall | 0.8289 |\n| F1 Score | 0.7892 |\n| Dice Score | 0.7892 |\n| IoU | 0.6625 |\n\n---\n\n# Final Test Results\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|----------|----------|\n| Accuracy | 0.9879 |\n| Precision | 0.7917 |\n| Recall | 0.8179 |\n| F1 Score | 0.7986 |\n| Dice Score | 0.7986 |\n| IoU | 0.6792 |\n\n---\n\n# Swin-T vs Swin-S Performance Comparison\n\n| Metric | Swin-T | Swin-S | Improvement |\n|----------|----------|----------|----------|\n| Precision | 0.7428 | 0.7917 | +4.9% |\n| Recall | 0.8006 | 0.8179 | +1.7% |\n| F1 Score | 0.7602 | 0.7986 | +3.8% |\n| Dice Score | 0.7602 | 0.7986 | +3.8% |\n| IoU | 0.6303 | 0.6792 | +4.9% |\n\n---\n\n# Why Swin-S Performs Better than Swin-T?\n\n## 1. Larger Model Capacity\n\n- Swin-S contains more parameters than Swin-T.\n- It can learn more complex landslide patterns.\n- Better feature representation capability.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Improved Feature Extraction\n\n- Swin-S captures richer spatial information.\n- More effective at identifying landslide boundaries.\n- Better understanding of texture and terrain variations.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Better Multi-Scale Learning\n\n- Landslides appear in different sizes and shapes.\n- Swin-S learns small and large landslide regions more effectively.\n- Improves segmentation quality.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Reduced False Positives\n\n- Precision improved from **0.7428 → 0.7917**.\n- Indicates fewer non-landslide regions are incorrectly classified as landslides.\n- Produces cleaner segmentation masks.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Improved Landslide Detection\n\n- Recall improved from **0.8006 → 0.8179**.\n- More actual landslide pixels are detected.\n- Better coverage of affected areas.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Better Segmentation Overlap\n\n- Dice Score improved from **0.7602 → 0.7986**.\n- Indicates stronger overlap between prediction and ground truth.\n- More accurate landslide localization.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Better Region-Level Segmentation\n\n- IoU improved from **0.6303 → 0.6792**.\n- Shows improved boundary alignment.\n- Predicted regions match ground truth more closely.\n\n---\n\n# Overall Conclusion\n\n- Swin-S consistently outperformed Swin-T across all evaluation metrics.\n- The largest improvements were observed in **Precision (+4.9%)** and **IoU (+4.9%)**.\n- The results indicate that increasing backbone capacity improves feature representation and segmentation quality.\n- DINOv2 + Mask2Former-Swin-S provides more accurate and robust landslide segmentation than DINOv2 + Mask2Former-Swin-T.\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n# Observations\n\n1. Validation and test metrics are highly consistent, indicating strong generalization capability.\n\n2. No significant evidence of data leakage was observed.\n\n3. Precision and recall remain well balanced, demonstrating reliable landslide detection.\n\n4. The Swin-S backbone significantly improved performance compared to the Swin-T backbone.\n\n5. Dice Score improved from approximately 0.760 to 0.799.\n\n6. IoU improved from approximately 0.630 to 0.679.\n\n7. The model achieved stable convergence with minimal overfitting.\n\n---\n\n# Conclusion\n\nThe DINOv2 + Mask2Former-Swin-S framework successfully performed landslide segmentation on the ISSLIDE dataset. The integration of transformer-based feature extraction, hybrid loss optimization, geometric data augmentation, adaptive learning rate scheduling, and early stopping produced strong segmentation performance and robust generalization. Compared to the Swin-T backbone, the Swin-S variant demonstrated superior feature representation and segmentation accuracy, making it a competitive architecture for landslide detection and a strong candidate for further research and comparison against SegFormer-B2 and future DINOv2-based implementations.","metadata":{},"attachments":{"b91975e0-7135-4cc4-ac23-2d01e0a2a4ba.png":{"image/png":""}}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import os\nimport cv2\nimport tifffile as tiff\nimport numpy as np\n\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\nimport albumentations as A\nfrom albumentations.pytorch import ToTensorV2\n\nfrom torch.utils.data import (\n    Dataset,\n    DataLoader,\n    random_split\n)\n\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nfrom sklearn.metrics import (\n    confusion_matrix,\n    ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n)\n\nfrom transformers import (\n    Mask2FormerForUniversalSegmentation,\n    AutoImageProcessor\n)\n\nfrom tqdm import tqdm","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-08T08:32:19.36976Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-08T08:32:19.370082Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.872551Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-08T08:32:19.370057Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.871619Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"code","source":"device = torch.device(\n\n    \"cuda\"\n\n    if torch.cuda.is_available()\n\n    else\n\n    \"cpu\"\n\n)\n\nprint(device)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.874531Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.874958Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.879536Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.874934Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.87872Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"cuda\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"ROOT_DIR = \"/kaggle/input/datasets/rishikeshgopal121/landslide/ReadyToBeUsedDataset\"","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.880558Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.881001Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.898306Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.880958Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-08T08:32:42.897556Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"code","source":"class DINOv2Dataset(Dataset):\n\n    def __init__(self, root_dir):\n\n   